estone":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/bazelbuild/bazel/milestones/5","html_url":"https://github.com/bazelbuild/bazel/milestone/5","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/bazelbuild/bazel/milestones/5/labels","id":1717213,"node_id":"MDk6TWlsZXN0b25lMTcxNzIxMw==","number":5,"title":"0.6","description":"","creator":{"login":"damienmg","id":3721087,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3MjEwODc=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3721087?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/damienmg","html_url":"https://github.com/damienmg","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/damienmg/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/damienmg/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/damienmg/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/damienmg/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/damienmg/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/damienmg/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/damienmg/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/damienmg/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/damienmg/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"open_issues":0,"closed_issues":158,"state":"closed","created_at":"2016-04-19T15:07:50Z","updated_at":"2020-05-12T11:10:23Z","due_on":"2017-06-30T00:00:00Z","closed_at":"2017-11-29T19:06:44Z"},"comments":0,"created_at":"2016-11-08T00:55:17Z","updated_at":"2017-03-27T11:39:15Z","closed_at":"2017-03-27T11:39:15Z","author_association":"NONE","type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Description of the problem / feature request / question:\r\n\r\nI want to use python multiprocessing module in a python test (python3.5), but it fails with OSError: [Error 30] Read-only file system. I think the following is the problem.\r\n\r\nwhen I run bazel test with --sandbox_tmpfs_path=/dev/shm/, I expect os.listdir(\"/dev/shm/\") to return [] and os.mkdir(\"/dev/shm/test\") to succeed (if I understand the --sandbox_tmpfs_path option correctly, it mounts a fresh writable filesystem at given path). however, what I got from os.listdir(\"/dev/shm/\") is the same list of files I got when I run that in a python interpreter without bazel/sandboxing, and os.mkdir(\"/dev/shm/test\") fails with OSError: [Error 30] Read-only file system: \"/dev/shm/test\".\r\n\r\nit seems that --sandbox_tmpfs_path is handling /dev/shm in a special way (maybe because it is already mounted? --sandbox_tmpfs_path /dev/shm seems to have no effect.). --sandbox_tmpfs_path /some/other/path seems to work as expected (i.e. os.listdir(\"/some/other/path\") returns [] and os.mkdir(\"/some/other/path/test\") succeeds).\r\n\r\n### Environment info\r\n* Operating System:\r\nUbuntu 14.04\r\n\r\n* Bazel version (output of `bazel info release`):\r\nrelease 0.4.0\r\n\r\n###  Have you found anything relevant by searching the web? (e.g. GitHub issues, email threads in the [bazel-discuss@googlegroups.com](https://groups.google.com/forum/#!forum/bazel-discuss) archive)\r\nrelated issues #1460 #1973\r\nthe suggestion in #1973 works, but still is the above the correct behavior for --sandbox_tmpfs_path=/dev/shm?\r\n\r\n","closed_by":{"login":"bazel-io","id":15028808,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE1MDI4ODA4","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/15028808?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/bazel-io","html_url":"https://github.com/bazel-io","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/bazel-io/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/bazel-io/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/bazel-io/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/bazel-io/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com